{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":75261,"output":599,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":75860,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"<|channel>thought\n<channel|>The `KeyError: 'merchant'` and the fact that `csv.DictReader` is returning only `['d']` as fieldnames suggests there might be a Byte Order Mark (BOM) or some other encoding issue at the beginning of the file, even though it looks like plain text.\n\nLet's try reading with `utf-8-sig` to handle potential BOMs and also check if there are any hidden characters in the header.\n\nI will use a more robust way to read the CSV by manually handling the header or using `csv.reader`.\n\nActually, let's look at the file content again very carefully.\nThe `head -c 10` showed `70 73 70 5f 72 65 66 65 72 65`, which is `p s p _ r e f e r`. No BOM there.\n\nWait, if `csv.DictReader` returns `['d']`, it means it's only seeing one column and the first character of that column is 'd'. This is very strange given the `head` output.\n\nLet's try reading the file with a different encoding or just using `csv.reader`.\n\nI will use `python3 -c \"import csv; reader = csv.reader('data/payments.csv'); print(next(reader))\"` again but I'll check if there are any weird characters.\n\nActually, let me try to read the first line and see what it really is.\n`cat -A data/payments.csv | head -n 1` showed `psp_reference,merchant,...$`.\n\nMaybe there's a carriage return `\\r`? `head -c 415` showed `...acquirer_country\\n`. No `\\r`.\n\nLet's try to use `pandas` if available, or just stick to `csv` but be very careful.\nI'll try to read it with `utf-8` and see if I can get the fieldnames correctly.\n\nWait, why did `python3 -c \"import csv; print(list(csv.DictReader('data/payments.csv').fieldnames))\"` return `['d']`?\nIf the file starts with `psp_reference`, then `merchant` is the second column.\nMaybe there's a w